-- title:
--   Local type at a prime exactly squared in the level
-- statement:
--   Let $M$ be a nonzero natural number and let $g$ be a weight-two cusp form on $\Gamma_0(M)$ that is a newform, i.e. $g$ is a normalised eigenform (its $q$-expansion coefficients satisfy $a_1=1$, multiplicativity at coprime arguments, and the usual recursions at prime powers, distinguishing primes dividing $M$ from those that do not) and no proper divisor $M'\mid M$ carries a normalised eigenform of weight two and level $M'$ whose $\ell$-th coefficients agree with those of $g$ for all primes $\ell\nmid M$. Let $\lambda$ be a prime, $S$ a finite set of naturals, and $O'$ a complete discrete valuation ring of characteristic zero with finite residue field in which $\lambda$ lies in the maximal ideal. Let $\chi$ be a ring homomorphism from the level-$M$ weight-two Hecke algebra away from $S$ (the $\mathbb{Z}$-subalgebra of $\operatorname{End}_{\mathbb C}S_2(\Gamma_0(M))$ generated by the $T_\ell$ for primes $\ell\nmid M$, $\ell\notin S$, and the $U_p$ for primes $p\mid M$, $p\notin S$) to $\mathbb C$ with $\chi(T_\ell)=a_\ell(g)$ for all such $\ell$, and let $\iota$ be a ring homomorphism from the image of $\chi$ to $O'$. Let $\rho$ be a two-dimensional adically continuous representation of $\operatorname{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb Q}/\mathbb Q)$ on a free $O'$-module of rank $2$ such that, for every prime $\ell\nmid M$ with $\ell\notin S$, every valuation subring $A$ of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ with $\ell$ a non-unit in $A$ and every $\sigma$ that is a Frobenius at $\ell$ for $A$, the characteristic polynomial of $\rho(\sigma)$ is $X^2-\iota(\chi(T_\ell))X+\ell$; assume the residual representation $\overline\rho$ on $k\otimes_{O'}V$, $k$ the residue field, admits no Galois-stable submodule other than $0$ and the whole module, and that $\det\overline\rho(c)=-1$ for every involution $c\neq 1$. Let $q\neq\lambda$ be a prime with $q$-adic valuation of $M$ equal to $2$, let $\lambda$ be odd, and assume $\overline\rho$, viewed as a representation over $k$, is unipotent on inertia at $q$: for every valuation subring $P$ of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ in which $q$ is a non-unit and every $\sigma$ in the inertia subgroup of $P$ over $\mathbb Q$, the characteristic polynomial of $\overline\rho(\sigma)$ is $(X-1)^2$. Let $\Phi$ be a nonzero complex-valued function on $\mathrm{GL}_2$ of the adeles of $\mathbb Q$ which is an adelic lift of $g$ (left invariant under the global points $\mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb Q)$, right invariant under the finite level-one subgroup at level $M$, and agreeing with the weight-two slash of $g$ evaluated at $i$ on points with trivial finite part and totally positive archimedean part). Assume finally the residual hypothesis for the eigensystem: for every complete discrete valuation ring $O''$ of characteristic zero with finite residue field that is a finite, local and injective $O'$-algebra, every two-dimensional adically continuous Galois representation over $O''$ whose Frobenius characteristic polynomials at the primes $\ell\neq\lambda$, $\ell\nmid M$, $\ell\notin S$ are $X^2-\iota(\chi(T_\ell))X+\ell$ (images taken in $O''$) has absolutely irreducible and odd residual representation. Then one of the following holds: either there are characters $\mu_1,\mu_2:\mathbb{Q}_q^\times\to\mathbb{C}^\times$ and a nonzero $\mathbb C$-linear, $\mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb{Q}_q)$-equivariant map from the span of the $\mathrm{GL}_2$-translates of $\Phi$ to the principal series attached to $(\mu_1,\mu_2)$ (locally constant functions on $\mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb{Q}_q)$ transforming under the Borel by $\mu_1\otimes\mu_2$ times the half-modulus), such that each $\mu_i$ has conductor exponent exactly $1$, $\mu_1^{-1}\mu_2$ is ramified, $\mu_1$ has $\lambda$-power order on the units of norm one, and $\lambda\mid q-1$; or every such equivariant map from that span into any principal series at $q$ vanishes and $\lambda\mid q+1$.
--
--   This is the local analysis at a prime $q$ with $q^2$ exactly dividing the level, in the level-lowering step: the local component at $q$ of the automorphic representation generated by $\Phi$ is either a ramified principal series whose characters satisfy a congruence forcing $\lambda\mid q-1$, or has no principal series quotient, in which case $\lambda\mid q+1$. It feeds the passage from a newform of level divisible by $q^2$ to one of smaller level, used by [`WeierstrassCurve.exists_isNormalizedEigenform_level_div_of_isNewform_of_factorization_eq_two`](thm.html#WeierstrassCurve.exists_isNormalizedEigenform_level_div_of_isNewform_of_factorization_eq_two).
